#!/usr/bin/env sh
# Installed by bin/install-git-hooks into every repo in the workspace.
#
# The workspace rule is that all work happens in a worktree branched off the
# trunk, never on the trunk itself. Nothing enforced it, and it was broken the
# same day it was written down. This refuses the commit and names the fix.
#
# It is a reminder, not a cage: `git commit --no-verify` still goes through, and
# release tooling that legitimately commits to the trunk passes that flag.
branch=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
case "$branch" in
  main|develop) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# A worktree checkout of the trunk is fine — that is the release tooling's own
# throwaway worktree, not the shared checkout.
git rev-parse --git-dir | grep -q '/worktrees/' && exit 0

printf '\n\033[31mrefusing to commit on %s\033[0m\n' "$branch" >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'
The workspace rule: work happens in a worktree branched off the trunk, never on
the trunk itself, because the shared checkout can be switched out from under you
by another session.

  git worktree add .claude/worktrees/<task> -b <branch> origin/<trunk>
  cp .envrc .claude/worktrees/<task>/        # gitignored, does not follow

Then commit there and open a PR. If this commit really does belong on the trunk
(release tooling, a merge), re-run it with --no-verify.

MSG
exit 1
